**I am retracting a sentence I filed two handoffs ago.** My
`plan__ta-anchor-ad5-contended-path-also-inherited-review-2b-refuted.md` concluded *"`AD5` is
inherited in full. No part of it is a PR-2 regression."* **The second half of that is wrong in the
regime Finding 68 §3 identified**, and review was right to say "in full" was ahead of the evidence.
Ran the experiment; reporting against my own conclusion.

## 1. The experiment, as specified

Finding 68 §3: at base, on the same fixture, set the dead entry's
`RoleCapacities[<any role>].RequiredCapacity` positive (decode alone suffices), leave `TotalDemand`
alone, re-run. I also honored both conditions it attached — `TotalDemand` untouched, and the path
instrumented. **Instrumentation is by construction rather than by counter:** rescale is off in the
steady rows, so `applyRescale` cannot run and `reclaimRole` is unreachable; the path is not in
doubt. Both optimizers, three starting heights, and I added the contended rows to close the matrix.

| regime | path | base `075a208e` | HEAD `a9afb740` |
|---|---|---|---|
| `deadRC = 0` (my earlier fixtures) | steady, rescale off | pf=**1** dc=n | pf=**1** dc=n |
| `deadRC = 0` | contended, rescale on | pf=**1** dc=n | pf=**1** dc=n |
| `deadRC > 0` (**under test**) | steady, rescale off | pf=**n** dc=**2n** | pf=**1** dc=n |
| `deadRC > 0` | contended, rescale on | pf=**n** dc=**2n** | pf=**1** dc=n |

n ∈ {2,4,8}, identical at every height; both optimizers agree on every row.

## 2. What it establishes

**Review's dispatch reading holds, and my "in full" does not.** The chain executes exactly as
Finding 68 read it: `s := votingResults(...)` → `initRoleState(s)` seeds `pickerState` from
`rc.RequiredCapacity` (`analyzer_helpers.go:385`) → `anyRoleNeedsScaleUp` scans **every ballot
entry** (`:709-718`), so one positive value anywhere flips the dispatch and the scale-down path is
never reached. At base, `votingResults` prunes on `Enabled` alone, so the dead entry's stale
`RequiredCapacity` is still on the ballot and prefill is **preserved at its starting count**. At
HEAD, `VG-up` (`952d2fff` — mine) prunes on `Enabled && Live`, the entry is gone, only TA's
structural zeros remain, and prefill drains to 1.

So: **in a reconcile where the outage entry carries a positive per-role `RequiredCapacity`, base does
not drain and HEAD does.** That is the realistic case, not the corner — an analyzer entry that has
just gone stale carries its last computed values; zero-everywhere is the artificial input, and it is
the one both of my earlier fixtures happened to supply.

Corrected attribution, by regime rather than by path:

- **`deadRC = 0`: inherited.** Base and HEAD identical, on both paths, all heights, twelve contended
  configurations. Unchanged, and now confirmed three separate ways.
- **`deadRC > 0`: HEAD-only.** `VG-up` opens the reachability of the drain. My commit is what makes
  it reachable in that regime.

**The contended row is also regime-sensitive, which review did not expect** (its reasoning was that
`applyRescale` never consults `anyRoleNeedsScaleUp`). One caveat I will not skip: base's contended
numbers are identical to its steady numbers, which suggests contention did not bite at
`limit = 3n, used = 2n, hog = 1000` and the value came from the sizing pass rather than from
`reclaimRole`. So that row is **not** discriminating for `reclaimRole` specifically; treat the
contended line as unresolved rather than as evidence.

## 3. What this does *not* mean, stated because the numbers invite the wrong reading

**Base is not correct here — it is differently wrong.** Base preserves prefill by scaling the whole
model **up to 2n** on the strength of a dead analyzer's stale figures. That is precisely the defect
`VG-up` was written to remove. The honest statement is that `VG-up` traded *acting on stale data* for
*exposing a pre-existing prefill-pricing hole* (`distributeDemandByRole`'s structural zero, which is
byte-identical at base). Both states are wrong; neither is a safe place to stand.

So this is **not** an argument to revert `VG-up`, and I am not making one. It does mean the deferral
rationale can no longer rest on "this is not PR-2's bug" for the `deadRC > 0` regime, because in that
regime the reachability is PR-2's. Which of the two wrongs is worse, and whether that moves `AD5`
from *defer* to *in scope*, is the planner's call and Dean's — not mine.

## 4. Floor-at-1: confirmed by mutation, and it is load-bearing elsewhere

Finding 68's answer is right, and I verified it by execution rather than accepting it. The clause is
`cost_aware_optimizer.go:157-161`, reached by both callers through the shared helper. Disabled it
(one-token mutation, applied and reverted, nothing committed) and re-ran the AD5 fixture:

| | prefill, clause live | prefill, clause disabled |
|---|---|---|
| steady (`scaleDownRoleIterated`) | 1 | **0** |
| contended (`reclaimRole`) | 1 | **0** |

n ∈ {2,4,8}, all six rows. So AD5's true severity absent that clause is **teardown of the prefill
role to zero**, and the only thing preventing it is a rule written as a *model-level* scale-to-zero
guard that happens to fire per-role because both callers pass `variantsForRole(...)` — a
single-variant set on a P/D model, so `i == len(sorted)-1` always holds and `sorted[:0]` is always
empty. The one surviving replica is an accident of that misapplication, not a designed floor.
Worth recording for whoever sizes an AD5 fix: **anything that touches the cheapest-at-1 rule's
positional test also removes AD5's last brake.** The mutation additionally failed 4 other specs in
386, so the clause is load-bearing beyond this fixture.

